+## agent
+
+This build's own group, with no upstream counterpart: what the Android app's chat view reads about a pane
+running Claude Code or Codex. It changes nothing in the app and has no UI; the tree and the snapshot never
+show it.
+
+`ngtermctl agent hook claude|codex EVENT [--target ID] [--pane P] [--pane-id TOKEN]` — run by the installed
+agent-status hooks on `SessionStart`, `UserPromptSubmit`, `PermissionRequest` and Claude's `SessionEnd`, with
+the hook's JSON payload on stdin. It sends `agent.report`, which keeps per pane, in memory only, the agent,
+its conversation id and transcript path, and the last permission prompt (shown as an approval only while the
+session's status is `blocked`). `SessionEnd` clears the record, as does the pane's foreground going back to
+its shell. Prints nothing on success. Don't run it by hand: it is the hooks' call, and a pane gets a record
+only after the hooks are reinstalled from this build and a new agent process starts; an ngterm restarted
+under a running agent learns it at the agent's next prompt.
+
+`ngtermctl agent chat [--target ID] [--pane left|right] [--limit N] [--before CURSOR | --after CURSOR]
+[--conversation ID]` — the pane's conversation as NDJSON, one chat event per line, each with `type`, `id`
+(a later line with the same id updates the item) and `cursor`: `conversation`, `user`, `assistant`,
+`thinking`, `tool`, `question`, `approval`, `approval_done`, `notice`, `unknown`, `reset` and `live`. By
+default it prints about the last `--limit` (200) events, then `live`, then follows the transcript; a new
+conversation in the pane is a `reset` and its backlog, and the record clearing ends it with exit 0. It also
+ends when stdin reaches its end, so give it a stdin that stays open (`</dev/null` prints the backlog and
+exits). `--before CURSOR` prints one older page and exits; `--after CURSOR` resumes from that cursor's line.
+
+`ngtermctl agent commands [--target ID] [--pane left|right]` — the agent's slash commands and skills as one
+JSON array of `{name, description, kind, argument_hint}` (`kind`: `builtin`, `skill`, `command` or
+`plugin`): the built-ins, the user's and the project's skills and custom commands, and those of each enabled
+plugin, read from the agent's own config dir (the one its transcript lies in). A Codex skill is named as
+Codex mentions it, `$name`.
+
+Both read the record through `agent.get` and fail with `no agent in this pane` when it has none.
+
